Part 1 — Prepare MongoDB
Step 1. Open PowerShell as Administrator
First, add MongoDB 8.3 to your PATH:
$env:Path += ";C:\Program Files\MongoDB\Server\8.3\bin"


Verify that MongoDB is accessible:
mongod --version


Also check:
mongos --version


Both commands should display version information.
Step 2. Create the required folders
Run these commands:
New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force C:\data\configdb
New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force C:\data\shard1
New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force C:\data\shard2


These folders will store the data for the three MongoDB servers.
Step 3. Check whether port 27017 is already occupied
Run:
Get-Service MongoDB


If the service is running, stop it before starting mongos on port 27017:
Stop-Service MongoDB


If the service doesn't exist, that's okay. Continue.

We need port 27017 available for the mongos router.

Part 2 — Start the config server
Step 4. Start the config server
Open a new PowerShell tab.

Run:
$env:Path += ";C:\Program Files\MongoDB\Server\8.3\bin"


Then:
mongod --configsvr --replSet configReplSet --dbpath C:\data\configdb --port 27019


Expected
 MongoDB starts and displays server logs.

Keep this terminal open. Do not close it.
Step 5. Initialize the config server
Open another PowerShell tab.

Run:
mongosh --port 27019


You will enter the MongoDB shell.

Now execute:
rs.initiate({
    _id: "configReplSet",
    configsvr: true,
    members: [
        { _id: 0, host: "localhost:27019" }
    ]
})


Wait a few seconds, then check:
rs.status()


Look for:
stateStr: "PRIMARY"


This means the config server's replica set is initialized.

Now exit:
exit


Keep the config server's original PowerShell terminal open.

Part 3 — Start Shard 1
Step 6. Start the first shard server
Open another PowerShell tab.

Run:
$env:Path += ";C:\Program Files\MongoDB\Server\8.3\bin"


Then:
mongod --shardsvr --replSet shard1Repl --dbpath C:\data\shard1 --port 27018


Keep this terminal open.
Step 7. Initialize Shard 1
Open another PowerShell tab:
mongosh --port 27018


Inside mongosh, execute:
rs.initiate({
    _id: "shard1Repl",
    members: [
        { _id: 0, host: "localhost:27018" }
    ]
})


Wait for initialization.

Check:
rs.status()


Look for PRIMARY.

Then:
exit



Part 4 — Start Shard 2
Step 8. Start the second shard server
Open another PowerShell tab.

Run:
$env:Path += ";C:\Program Files\MongoDB\Server\8.3\bin"


Then:
mongod --shardsvr --replSet shard2Repl --dbpath C:\data\shard2 --port 27020


Keep this terminal open.
Step 9. Initialize Shard 2
Open another PowerShell tab:
mongosh --port 27020


Execute:
rs.initiate({
    _id: "shard2Repl",
    members: [
        { _id: 0, host: "localhost:27020" }
    ]
})


Wait for initialization.

Check:
rs.status()


Look for PRIMARY.

Then:
exit



Part 5 — Start the Mongos Router
Step 10. Start mongos
Open another PowerShell tab.

Run:
$env:Path += ";C:\Program Files\MongoDB\Server\8.3\bin"


Then execute:
mongos --configdb configReplSet/localhost:27019 --port 27017


Keep this terminal open.

What is mongos?

mongos is the router that receives database queries and forwards them to the appropriate shard.
Step 11. Connect to the sharded cluster
Open one final PowerShell tab.

Execute:
mongosh --port 27017


You are now connected to the cluster through mongos.

Add both shards:
sh.addShard("shard1Repl/localhost:27018")


sh.addShard("shard2Repl/localhost:27020")


Check the cluster:
sh.status()


You should see both shard names:
- shard1Repl
- shard2Repl
Sharded cluster initialized

Part 6 — Create the database and insert records
From now on, execute the database commands in the same mongosh window connected to port 27017.
Step 12. Create/select the database
use perfTestDB


This selects the database named perfTestDB.
Step 13. Create the unsharded collection
db.movies_unsharded.drop()


This removes an existing collection with that name, if present.

Now insert one million movie records:
for (let i = 0; i < 1000000; i++) {
    db.movies_unsharded.insertOne({
        title: "Movie " + i,
        genre: (i % 5 === 0) ? "Action" : "Drama",
        year: 2000 + (i % 20),
        rating: Math.random() * 10
    })
}


Wait until it finishes. Inserting one million documents individually can take several minutes or longer.
Step 14. Enable sharding for the database
sh.enableSharding("perfTestDB")


This enables sharding for the database.
Step 15. Create the sharded collection
db.movies_sharded.drop()


Now define the shard key:
sh.shardCollection("perfTestDB.movies_sharded", { year: 1 })


Here:
- movies_sharded is the collection.
- year is the shard key.
- 1 means ascending order.
Step 16. Insert one million records into the sharded collection
Execute:
for (let i = 0; i < 1000000; i++) {
    db.movies_sharded.insertOne({
        title: "Movie " + i,
        genre: (i % 5 === 0) ? "Action" : "Drama",
        year: 2000 + (i % 20),
        rating: Math.random() * 10
    })
}


Wait until it finishes.

Part 7 — Compare query performance
Now we compare the two collections using MongoDB's explain() method.
Step 17. Equality query
First, run this for the unsharded collection:
db.movies_unsharded.find({ year: 2010 })
.explain("executionStats").executionStats


Then run this for the sharded collection:
db.movies_sharded.find({ year: 2010 })
.explain("executionStats").executionStats


Compare these three values:
Field	Meaning
executionTimeMillis	Time taken by the query
totalDocsExamined	Documents examined
nReturned	Documents returned

Step 18. Range query
Unsharded collection:
db.movies_unsharded.find({
    year: { $gte: 2010, $lt: 2015 }
}).explain("executionStats").executionStats


Sharded collection:
db.movies_sharded.find({
    year: { $gte: 2010, $lt: 2015 }
}).explain("executionStats").executionStats


This query retrieves movies from 2010 to 2014.
Step 19. Aggregation query
Unsharded collection:
db.movies_unsharded.aggregate([
    { $match: { year: { $gte: 2010, $lt: 2015 } } },
    {
        $group: {
            _id: "$genre",
            avgRating: { $avg: "$rating" }
        }
    }
]).explain("executionStats")


Sharded collection:
db.movies_sharded.aggregate([
    { $match: { year: { $gte: 2010, $lt: 2015 } } },
    {
        $group: {
            _id: "$genre",
            avgRating: { $avg: "$rating" }
        }
    }
]).explain("executionStats")


This calculates the average movie rating for each genre.
Step 20. Check shard distribution
Run:
sh.status()


You can also inspect the collection's distribution:
db.movies_sharded.getShardDistribution()


This helps you understand how the documents are distributed across shards.
